    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're absolutely right to dig deeper into the cost implications, as the \"hidden\" fees and transaction frictions are often where the real money gets lost or saved. While ETFs generally boast lower expense ratios due to their passive, index-tracking nature, they can still incur trading costs if actively managed, whereas mutual funds often have higher management fees but sometimes offer lower minimum investment thresholds that can be a relief for smaller portfolios.\n\nHere is how the trading mechanics and management styles directly impact your client's bottom line:\n\n| Cost Factor | ETFs (Exchange Traded Funds) | Mutual Funds |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Expense Ratios** | **Generally Lower:** Passive ETFs often charge between 0.03% and 0.20% annually. Active ETFs are higher but still frequently undercut mutual funds. | **Generally Higher:** Active management fees typically range from 0.50% to over 2.00% annually, covering the manager's salary and research. |\n| **Trading Friction** | **Bid-Ask Spread:** Investors pay the difference between the bid and ask price; for illiquid ETFs, this can be significant. | **No",
      "source": "target",
